  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Demonstration of community-based conservation in the Darin by engaging two indigenous Ember-.Wounaan communities in reforestation and development of alternative incomes that promote.conservation.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The purpose of this project is to engage two indigenous Ember-Wounaan communities in alternative farming and grazing systems to promote community-based biodiversity conservation in multiple use zones around Darin National Park, Panama. The project is intended to conserve Darins lowland moist tropical forest and unique Choco biodiversity, including Central Americas largest harpy eagle population, by reducing slash-and-burn agriculture, pastureland conversion, and overall dependence on forest resources and bush meat. Specific activities include: (1) public campaign to build a conservation constituency in the region, (2) reforestation with native trees, native commercially valuable trees, and shade-grown coffee; (3) raising pigs as an alternative to bush meat and new swift revenue source until shade-coffee plantations reach maturation; (4) creation of two local community associations and one national NGO to increase capacity to sign and manage incentive agreements; (5) training in administration and management of associations to support good community forest governance; (6) create a student scholarship program as an incentive for families participating in the reforestation initiative; and (7) building a coalition of national and international partners for the Darin to support policy coherence for the region and to scale up community-conservation efforts across the landscape.</narrative>
  </description>
